{"name":"Old Flame","hex":"#f2b7b5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old flame","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A previous boyfriend or girlfriend; a former romantic or sexual partner, especially one for whom one still has romantic feelings."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old_flame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Old Flame (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Old Flame is the seventh studio album by American pop and country singer Juice Newton. It was released in October 1985 by RCA Records."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/64/Cover_for_the_album_Old_Flame_by_Juice_Newton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Flame_(album)","wikidata":"Q28451248"},{"title":"Old Flames","blocks":[{"text":"Old Flames is a studio album by jazz saxophonist Sonny Rollins, released on the Milestone label in 1993, featuring performances by Rollins with Clifton Anderson, Tommy Flanagan, Bob Cranshaw and Jack DeJohnette with Jon Faddis, Byron Stripling, Alex Brofsky and Bob Stewart added on two tracks which were arranged by Jimmy Heath."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/ba/Old_Flames_%28Sonny_Rollins%29_coverart.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Flames","wikidata":"Q3350190"},{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/330px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame","wikidata":"Q235544"},{"title":"Scarlet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Scarlet is a bright red color, sometimes with a slightly orange tinge. In the spectrum of visible light, and on the traditional color wheel, it is one-quarter of the way between red and orange, slightly less orange than vermilion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/RCMP-female-officer.jpg/330px-RCMP-female-officer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarlet_(color)","wikidata":"Q2268159"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/1280px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spectrum of the blue (premixed, i.e., complete combustion) flame from a butane torch showing molecular radical band emission and Swan bands. Virtually all the light produced is in the blue to green region of the spectrum below about 565 nanometers, accounting for the bluish color of sootless hydrocarbon flames.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/1280px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different flame types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In zero-G, convection does not carry the hot combustion p